explanations.summary:
The proof replaces the two variables by independent copies on a common probability space, preserving their laws. On that space the Ruzsa distance is rewritten as the entropy of the difference minus half the two individual entropies. The desired absolute-value inequality then reduces to showing that each of $H[X';
u]$ and $H[Y';
u]$ is at most $H[X'-Y';
u]$, which is exactly the max-entropy bound for independent variables; linear arithmetic finishes the two sides.

