explanations.summary:
The proof follows the entropic BSG strategy. Replace $(A,B)$ by two conditionally independent copies $(A_1,B_1)$, $(A_2,B_2)$ on a new space, both glued to a common variable $Z'=A_1+B_1=A_2+B_2$ having the law of $A+B$. The integrated Ruzsa distance over the conditioning variable $Z$ is invariant under this replacement, and by conditional independence it equals $H[A_1-B_2\mid Z']-	frac12H[A_1\mid Z']-	frac12 H[B_2\mid Z']$. The first term is controlled by the triple entropy inequality: $H[\langle A_1,\langle B_1,A_1-B_2angleangle;
u]+H[A_1-B_2;
u]\le H[\langle A_1,A_1-B_2angle;
u]+H[\langle B_1,A_1-B_2angle;
u]$, whose right side is at most $2H[A]+2H[B]$; conditional independence evaluates the left-side triple entropy as $2H[\langle A,Bangle]-H[A+B]$, which turns the bound into $H[A_1-B_2\mid Z']\le 2I[A:B]+H[A+B]$. The two remaining conditional entropies are computed by the chain rule as $H[A]+H[B]-I[A:B]-H[A+B]$, and the final arithmetic yields the stated inequality.

